[
    {
        "content": "<p>We currently have three different ways to express the multiplicity of a prime divisor:<br>\n<a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Nat.factorization#doc\">docs#Nat.factorization</a><br>\n<a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=padicValNat#doc\">docs#padicValNat</a><br>\n<a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=multiplicity#doc\">docs#multiplicity</a><br>\nIs it possible to combine these at all? One option would be to have <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=multiplicity#doc\">docs#multiplicity</a> replace <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=padicValNat#doc\">docs#padicValNat</a>, and add a <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Finsupp#doc\">docs#Finsupp</a>-valued multiplicity to replace <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Nat.factorization#doc\">docs#Nat.factorization</a> (if we can get rid of <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=padicValInt#doc\">docs#padicValInt</a> in the same go, that would be even better).</p>\n<p>One issue is that <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=multiplicity#doc\">docs#multiplicity</a> junk-values to <code>1</code> rather than <code>0</code>. Is there a good reason for this?</p>",
        "id": 621088052,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Browning",
        "timestamp": 1788368116
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><code>multiplicity</code> is more generally typed, so only <code>Nat.factorization</code> and <code>padicValNat</code> can sensibly be combined. I am personally in favour of merging <code>padicValNat</code> into <code>Nat.factorization</code> since the latter is lower-level.</p>",
        "id": 621089369,
        "sender_full_name": "Jeremy Tan",
        "timestamp": 1788368395
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>There's <a class=\"stream-topic\" data-stream-id=\"287929\" href=\"/#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/Valued.2C.20valuation.2C.20valuative.2C.20.2E.2E.2E/with/599564483\">#mathlib4 &gt; Valued, valuation, valuative, ...</a> and I think another thread about this deprecation</p>",
        "id": 621090779,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1788368686
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>So it seems that we should nuke <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=emultiplicity%3F#doc\">docs#emultiplicity?</a></p>",
        "id": 621107110,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Browning",
        "timestamp": 1788371927
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>But it also seems to me that if we manage to switch <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=multiplicity#doc\">docs#multiplicity</a> to junk-value to <code>0</code>, then we could safely deprecate <code>padicValNat</code>, no?</p>",
        "id": 621107287,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Browning",
        "timestamp": 1788371961
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I also found <a class=\"stream-topic\" data-stream-id=\"217875\" href=\"/#narrow/channel/217875-Is-there-code-for-X.3F/topic/Multiplicity.20in.20the.20naturals/with/503508259\">#Is there code for X? &gt; Multiplicity in the naturals</a></p>",
        "id": 621443674,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1788452269
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I’m strongly in favour of merging many of these!</p>",
        "id": 621878337,
        "sender_full_name": "David Ang",
        "timestamp": 1788595731
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Ok, here's a plan:<br>\n1) Refactor <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=multiplicity#doc\">docs#multiplicity</a> to have <code>0</code> as a junk value (<a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/43573\">#43573</a>)<br>\n2) Deprecate <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=padicValNat#doc\">docs#padicValNat</a> in favor of multiplicity (and maybe also <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=padicValInt#doc\">docs#padicValInt</a>)<br>\n3) Deprecate <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=emultiplicity#doc\">docs#emultiplicity</a><br>\n(2 and 3 can be done in parallel)</p>",
        "id": 622539024,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Browning",
        "timestamp": 1788873595
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Could we keep the <code>Nat</code> &amp; <code>Int</code> versions under <code>Nat.multiplicity</code> &amp; <code>Int.multiplicity</code>? It's useful that they're computable, when testing things out with <code>#eval</code>.<br>\nAlso we should definitely keep <code>Nat.factorization</code> computable IMO.</p>",
        "id": 622540947,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1788874017
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Would something like <code>@[csimp]</code> help here? It would be nice to avoid the duplication if possible.</p>",
        "id": 622541391,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Browning",
        "timestamp": 1788874118
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Why would we need <code>Nat.multiplicity</code> if we already have <code>Nat.factorization</code>?</p>",
        "id": 622542083,
        "sender_full_name": "Yaël Dillies",
        "timestamp": 1788874223
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Currently <code>Nat.factorization</code> is defined using <code>padicValNat</code></p>",
        "id": 622542182,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1788874245
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>That would obviously be changed under Thomas' plan</p>",
        "id": 622542569,
        "sender_full_name": "Yaël Dillies",
        "timestamp": 1788874322
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"253861\">Thomas Browning</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/Deprecating.20.60padicValNat.60.3F/near/622541391\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Would something like <code>@[csimp]</code> help here? It would be nice to avoid the duplication if possible.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Yeah if it's possible and <code>Decidable</code> instances still work then sure</p>",
        "id": 622542795,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1788874369
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"387244\">Yaël Dillies</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/Deprecating.20.60padicValNat.60.3F/near/622542569\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>That would obviously be changed under Thomas' plan</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Oh then I agree. I guess we could either inline the current definition of <code>padicValNat</code> into <code>Nat.factorization</code>, or define it using <code>n.primeFactorsList.count</code>.</p>\n<p>btw if we ever get computable polynomials I'd want to have a computable <code>multiplicity</code> on them, so we would have a bit of duplication.</p>",
        "id": 622543815,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1788874611
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Ah, <code>n.primeFactorsList.count</code> seems like a good solution for now.</p>",
        "id": 622544419,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Browning",
        "timestamp": 1788874729
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Does csimp work when specializing a generic construction to a specific type?</p>",
        "id": 622544774,
        "sender_full_name": "Yakov Pechersky",
        "timestamp": 1788874809
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Apparently not? Here's the <code>primefactorsList.count</code> solution: <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/43581\">#43581</a></p>",
        "id": 622561285,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Browning",
        "timestamp": 1788878089
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>As another alternative we could make <code>[e]multiplicity</code> require <code>Decidable (FiniteMultiplicity a b)</code>, which <code>Nat</code> and <code>Int</code> <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/RingTheory/Multiplicity.html#Nat.decidableFiniteMultiplicity\">both have</a>, but that might make it a bit more annoying to use in other places.</p>",
        "id": 622572461,
        "sender_full_name": "Snir Broshi",
        "timestamp": 1788880428
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"253861\">Thomas Browning</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/Deprecating.20.60padicValNat.60.3F/near/622539024\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Ok, here's a plan:<br>\n1) Refactor <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=multiplicity#doc\">docs#multiplicity</a> to have <code>0</code> as a junk value<br>\n2) Deprecate <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=padicValNat#doc\">docs#padicValNat</a> in favor of multiplicity (and maybe also <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=padicValInt#doc\">docs#padicValInt</a>)<br>\n3) Deprecate <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=emultiplicity#doc\">docs#emultiplicity</a><br>\n(2 and 3 can be done in parallel)</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>(1) is done now. For (2), I have <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/43722\">#43722</a> which replaces almost all occurrences of <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=padicValNat#doc\">docs#padicValNat</a> with <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=mutiplicity#doc\">docs#mutiplicity</a>. The diff is a bit large (+427/-277 touching 14 files), so speedy reviews would be very welcome. A full deprecation will require a bit more refactoring which will be done in a followup PR.</p>",
        "id": 623754262,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Browning",
        "timestamp": 1789227047
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"253861\">Thomas Browning</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/Deprecating.20.60padicValNat.60.3F/near/622561285\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Apparently not? Here's the <code>primefactorsList.count</code> solution: <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/43581\">#43581</a></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Urgh, this is not well-founded recursion? There is an unfortunate situation here:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"c1\">-- `padicValNat p n` is `(Nat.maxPowDvdDiv p n).1`, a well-founded recursion by repeated squaring</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">-- of `p`, and `padicValInt p z` is `padicValNat p z.natAbs`. Both reduce in less than `0.1`</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">-- seconds, also for `3 ^ 1000`, so their only defect is the deprecation in favour of</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">-- `multiplicity`.</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">padicValNat</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">3</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">3</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">^</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">40</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">^</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">127</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">40</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">decide</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"n\">kernel</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- `Nat.factorization n p` is `if p.Prime then padicValNat p n else 0`, so it is as fast today, but</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">-- it is about to be redefined as `n.primeFactorsList.count p`.</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">factorization</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">3</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">^</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">40</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">^</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">127</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">))</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">3</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">40</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">decide</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"n\">kernel</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- `n.primeFactorsList.count p` factors all of `n` by `minFac` trial division, so its cost grows</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">-- with the square root of the largest prime factor: `0.15`, `0.16`, `0.3` and `5` to `7` seconds</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">-- for the cofactors `7`, `1009`, `1000003` and `2 ^ 31 - 1`, while for `2 ^ 61 - 1` or</span>\n<span class=\"c1\">-- `2 ^ 127 - 1` the kernel does not finish within minutes.</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">primeFactorsList</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">3</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">^</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">40</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">^</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">31</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">)))</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">count</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">3</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">40</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">decide</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"n\">kernel</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>I'm in an older version where <code>Nat.factorization</code> is still in terms of <code>padicValNat</code>.</p>",
        "id": 625960741,
        "sender_full_name": "David Ang",
        "timestamp": 1790066570
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"246273\">@Bhavik Mehta</span> is probably going to say this is a non-issue, but now we don't have a fast way to do kernel-computable p-adic valuations</p>",
        "id": 625961120,
        "sender_full_name": "David Ang",
        "timestamp": 1790066671
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Oh I guess I didn't read the conversation on GitHub, so we could just use <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Data/Nat/MaxPowDiv.html#Nat.maxPowDvdDiv\">Nat.maxPowDvdDiv</a> instead?</p>",
        "id": 625962036,
        "sender_full_name": "David Ang",
        "timestamp": 1790066858
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"464700\">David Ang</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/Deprecating.20.60padicValNat.60.3F/near/625962036\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Oh I guess I didn't read the conversation on GitHub, so we could just use <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/Mathlib/Data/Nat/MaxPowDiv.html#Nat.maxPowDvdDiv\">Nat.maxPowDvdDiv</a> instead?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Yes, exactly. Note that <code>Nat.factorization n</code> (without plugging in a prime) already requires factoring <code>n</code>.</p>",
        "id": 625962287,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Browning",
        "timestamp": 1790066911
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>And presumably that's not going to be deprecated following padicValNat? Seems a bit hard to find</p>",
        "id": 625962989,
        "sender_full_name": "David Ang",
        "timestamp": 1790067066
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Yes, correct.</p>",
        "id": 625963081,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Browning",
        "timestamp": 1790067089
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>(Although at this point <code>padicValNat</code> hasn't been touched yet since the big refactor <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/43722\">#43722</a> is still waiting on review)</p>",
        "id": 625963245,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Browning",
        "timestamp": 1790067131
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The reason we're deprecating padicValNat/Int was to unify the three (out of ten?) different notions right? Now there's a fourth notion I didn't know about... so what's the reason for keeping this and not padicValNat/Int (and the others)?</p>",
        "id": 625964862,
        "sender_full_name": "David Ang",
        "timestamp": 1790067499
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The main goal is to reduce the number of different ways to spell the same thing in theorem statements. <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=padicValNat#doc\">docs#padicValNat</a> is being deprecated because it is a duplicate of <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=multiplicity#doc\">docs#multiplicity</a>. <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Nat.factorization#doc\">docs#Nat.factorization</a> has the advantage of being a <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Finsupp#doc\">docs#Finsupp</a>, but should only be used in theorem statements if you really do need the <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Finsupp#doc\">docs#Finsupp</a> (as soon as you plug in a value, the simp-normal-form becomes <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=multiplicity#doc\">docs#multiplicity</a> via <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Nat.factorization_def#doc\">docs#Nat.factorization_def</a>). <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Nat.maxPowDvdDiv#doc\">docs#Nat.maxPowDvdDiv</a> has the advantage of efficient computation (it's fine to keep around, but shouldn't be used in theorem statements).</p>",
        "id": 625967599,
        "sender_full_name": "Thomas Browning",
        "timestamp": 1790068140
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Not in theorem statements but we may still want to link it with other definitions :)</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">lemma</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Nat</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">maxPowDvdDiv_fst_eq_multiplicity</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hp</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hn</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≠</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">maxPowDvdDiv</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"m\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">multiplicity</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">have</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span c